{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '180,260p' collab/1792050511876-architect-8f1-ruling.report.md; cat collab/1792052228000-architect-8f1-review.report.md; rg -n '8f|D1|D2' collab/1792560000000-architect-phase8-plan.report.md; rg -n '\"'^### 8.4|''^### 8.5'\"' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md; cat docs/agents/build-n-test.md\"","aggregated_output":"Completed-GMT: 2026-10-15 08:17:08 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-15 15:17:08 +07\nCoding-Agent: claude (claude-fable-5-1)\nSession-ID: pending (claude -p; the harness does not expose it)\n\n# 8f-1 Architect review of the uncommitted diff on srvm-phase8f1 @ d43afbe3\n\nRead-only; this report is the only artifact. Everything below was re-verified against the working tree and HEAD with git/grep, not taken from the engineer reports.\n\n## 1. What I checked\n\n| gate | result |\n|---|---|\n| Steps 0-5 of the ruling, file by file | every prescribed edit present; deviations D1-D3 only (verdicts in §2) |\n| Three pinned descriptor/golden literals | byte-identical at HEAD and in the working tree: `7b7c6ae9…f3489` (`debruijn_code_test:362`), `aa757659…7e74c` (`debruijn_register_code_test:11`), four golden H values (`debruijn_code_test:314-321`); `debruijn_register_code_test.cljc` wholly unchanged (`git diff --quiet` exit 0) |\n| Literal 23-mnemonic table vs the derivation | compared entry by entry against HEAD `yin.vm.code/vector-operand-table` (22 rows, `code.cljc:226-247`): the 20 carried rows match slot count, order, kind and name with only the namespace moved to `yin.debruijn.code`; `:var` is absent; `:load-bound`, `:load-free`, `:closure` are the three redefined rows with the same slots as the old inline literal. 20 + 3 = 23. `terminators` `#{:jump :return :halt}` equals HEAD `code/terminators` (`code.cljc:26-28`). No gap; the passing `descriptor-declares-the-contract` pin is the independent proof |\n| Deftest name-set diff (D3) | see §2 D3; nothing vanished beyond the ledger |\n| Scope | `git status`: no `src/yang`, no `docs/`, no `.md`; the only C4 file is `test/yang/python/antlr/int_heap_test.cljc` (require + first `run-all` block, as ruled) |\n| Removed v3 facts live where ruled | `scan_test/assert-agreement` keeps `(is (= (:free-names new) tree-names))` and `(is (= (:requirements new) (vm/ast-requirements rows)))`; `cell_test/lift-of-a-bare-cell-ref-is-refused-test` present; `debruijn_code_test:627-629` inlines the three FFI pair keys; `publish_test` asserts the manifest's footprint effects directly |\n| Leftover references to deleted code | none: `yin.vm.completion`, `segment-requirements`, `segment-extraction-queries`, `ucf.handoff`, `ucf.checkpoint`, `renamespace-slots`, `carried-mnemonics` have no code reader under `src test`. The `completion/` hits under `yin/vm/ucf/authority/*` are the unrelated `yin.vm.ucf.authority.completion` alias (`front.cljc:108`). Two stale docstrings remain (P3 below) |\n| `ast-requirements` docstring | byte-identical to HEAD; it never had the \"segment twin\" sentence my ruling told the engineer to drop. Engineer's call to leave it is correct |\n| Hygiene | `git diff --check` clean; index empty; deletions are filesystem unlinks (orchestrator: `git add -A` on the listed paths stages them as deletions) |\n\n## 2. Deviation verdicts\n\n**D1 — `vm/opcode-table` and `opcase` not deleted: CONFIRMED.** My ruling R3 was wrong to put them in step 3. `semantic.cljc` reads `vm/opcode-table` at :563, :624, :634, :760, :767, :768, :770 and `semantic_test.cljc:118-125` reads it six times; `opcase` is `semantic.cljc`'s only. Deleting them in 8f-1 cannot compile while `semantic.cljc` is retained. They die in 8f-3 in the same commit as `semantic.cljc`, `semantic_test` and `linearize.cljc`. The 8f-3 ledger gains: `vm.cljc:612-680` (`opcode-table` :619, `opcase` :647). The optimisation note `yin/vm/docs/bytecode-vm-optimization.md:336` that mentions `vm/opcase` is 8g's.\n\n**D2 — `query_test/segment-db` kept for `code-joins-store-put-key-to-ast-by-value`: CONFIRMED, with the disposition for 8f-3 fixed now.** My ruling enumerated two callers; the third is real (`query_test:1855-1866`). The old-code dependency in that test is exactly two things: `code/project-segment-qualified` (the v3 positional segment projection) and `linearize/lower-rows` (the v3 lowerer), both in `segment-db` (`query_test:1813-1818`). The test itself asserts a dao.space fact (U6: a `$ast`/`$code` join by value across relations that share no ids), not an old-kernel fact, so it must be PORTED in 8f-3, not dropped. The port is mechanical: `segment-db` becomes `(rel (scan/segment-rows (:vector (sr-linearize/project ast))))` (`scan/segment-rows` is the v4 twin, ruling fact 6), and because the register `:store-put` row carries `rd` before `key` (`semantic_register/code.cljc:45`, row shape `[a pc :store-put rd key value]`) the pattern changes from `[$code _ _ :store-put ?key _]` to `[$code _ _ :store-put _ ?key & _]`. With that, the `yin.vm.code` and `yin.vm.linearize` requires of `query_test` go in 8f-3. Retaining instead of porting in 8f-1 is the right call under minimal diff: 8f-3 already edits `query_test` for the same two requires, and a new v4 join assertion belongs in the slice whose three-host lane validates the register path.\n\n**D3 — completion_test had 18 deftests, not 19: CONFIRMED; nothing else vanished.** `git show HEAD:test/yin/vm/completion_test.cljc` contains 18 `(deftest` forms; my 19 was a miscount. Name-set diff over `test/**/*.{clj,cljc,cljs,cljd}` (HEAD vs working tree, same extraction both sides, 4396 → 4374 by my regex; the engineer's regex gave 4440 → 4418; both deltas −22):\n\n- removed, 24 names: the 18 completion_test names (`blocked-reader-wait-entry-is-a-root`, `caller-fetch-answers-what-the-vm-does-not-hold`, `code-facts-from-tree-and-segment-are-equal`, `code-side-missing-pid-is-incomplete`, `def-recursion-terminates`, `early-refusals`, `env-binding-does-not-discharge-a-free-name`, `ffi-pair-key-operand-is-refused`, `host-functions-in-values`, `loaded-images-answer-fetch`, `module-names`, `present-parked-record-is-pulled`, `primitive-without-a-profile-is-incomplete`, `segment-free-names-cases`, `stored-closure-is-a-work-item-and-its-names-are-discharged`, `two-closures-one-segment-two-envs`, `unfetchable-segment-blocks-and-the-rest-is-walked`, `value-side-missing-pid-is-refused`); the four single deletions `canonicalize-verifies-the-batch-stamp` (rule_r), `completion-address-reuse-preserves-answers` (scan), `completion-over-a-cell-is-not-complete-test` (cell), `requirement-sets-from-tree-and-segment-are-equal` (query); and the two renamed names `footprint-mnemonics-is-the-v3-table-minus-push`, `opcode-table-derives-from-the-named-table`.\n- added, 2 names: `footprint-mnemonics-is-the-v4-table`, `opcode-table-is-this-dimensions-own-data`.\n\n24 − 2 = 22. Every removed name is accounted for by the ruling's ledger; no deftest outside it disappeared.\n\n## 3. Findings\n\n**P1 (blocking):** none.\n\n**P2 (should fix before commit):** none.\n\n**P3 (nits; fix in 8f-1 if the orchestrator wants a clean slice, otherwise ledgered):**\n\n1. `test/yin/vm/linker/publish_test.cljc:173` — the deftest is still named `published-footprint-completes-module-discovery` but now asserts only that the published manifest carries `[:yin.module/footprint :effects]` = `#{}`. The name describes the dropped `completion/complete` fact. Suggested name: `published-manifest-carries-the-footprint-effects`.\n2. `test/yin/vm/debruijn_code_test.cljc:338,345` — `(is (not (contains? dc/opcode-table :var)))` appears twice (my ruling prescribed it in the new first block while the kept second block already had it). Harmless duplication; drop the first.\n3. `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf.cljc:326` — the `parking-kinds` docstring names `yin.vm.ucf.handoff`, deleted in this slice. R6 says a docstring naming a deleted var is fixed in the deleting step; `parking-kinds` itself dies in 8f-3 step 4b, so deletion there is an acceptable fix. Ledger it for 8f-3 (and 8g's grep gate) if not touched now.\n4. `test/yin/vm/linker_test.cljc:263,315` — prose says `yin.vm.code/materialize-vector!` / `yin.vm.code`; the file's `code` alias is already `yin.vm.semantic-register.code` (:22). My R4 missed these two prose sites. Fix with 8f-3's `yin.vm.code` deletion (or in the 8f-2 rename, where the alias target moves anyway).\n5. `src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf/rows/checkpoint.cljc:4` says it \"replaces `yin.vm.ucf.checkpoint`\", now historical prose. 8g.\n\nVerification status as reported by the engineer and not re-run here: JVM quick tier 4068 tests / 251041 assertions / 0 failures with `-e :slow`, kondo diagnostics unchanged against a frozen baseline. Node and Dart lanes are unrun; the three-host lane (`pinned-identities-are-host-independent`, `register-determinism`) is the ruling's landing gate and the standing commit rule (sign-off + green lanes), so the orchestrator runs them before committing. Nothing in this diff is host-specific beyond what those lanes already cover.\n\n## 4. Ledger additions for 8f-3 (from this review)\n\n- Delete `vm.cljc` `opcode-table` (:619) and `opcase` (:647) with `semantic.cljc` (D1).\n- Port `query_test/segment-db` to `scan/segment-rows` over `sr-linearize/project` and change the join pattern to `[$code _ _ :store-put _ ?key & _]`; then drop `yin.vm.code` and `yin.vm.linearize` from `query_test` (D2).\n- Fix or delete the stale prose at `ucf.cljc:326`, `linker_test.cljc:263,315`.\n\n## 5. Invariants\n\nA is the only code identity: unchanged, H stays a derived checksum. Tree always available: completion's tree-less segment scoping is what died; the scanner's agreement tests still compare against the tree. Derive, don't persist: the one new literal replaces a derivation whose source dies; no new table beyond the private `terminators` set. Minimal diff: 73 insertions against 5794 deletions, zero new namespaces. No backend widening: 23 mnemonics in, 23 out, `lowering-contract-version` stays 2.\n\nSIGN-OFF\n20:11. Irreversible order: regenerate goldens and move the oracle (8a) before deleting the old lowerers (8e); switch the production pipeline (8c) before deleting; delete the old kernel (8f) last, after the C4 suite is green over new images.\n99:| 5 + 1 segment addresses | `int_literal_test.cljc` (5), `float_address_test.cljc:227-269` (1 + 4 fragments) | A addresses of Python literals under the OLD v3 vector? To verify at 8f: if these are tree or v4 addresses they hold; if v3 vector addresses they die with v3 | verify, regenerate only if they are v3 (C4 files, Q1) |\n119:Deftest counts at stake: old-kernel direct tests 91 (`semantic_test`, `semantic_engine_test`, `semantic_ffi_test`, `semantic_stream_observer_test`, `linearize_test`, `code_test`); old lowerer tests 85 (`debruijn_resolve_test`, `debruijn_linearize_test`, `debruijn_register_compile_test`, `debruijn_vm_contract_test`, `debruijn_register_contract_test`, `debruijn_register_benchmark_test`); old transport tests 141. Total suite today 4574 deftests. Of the 91, `semantic_engine_test`, `semantic_ffi_test`, `semantic_stream_observer_test` are engine-level tests parameterised over the old kernel and should be **ported** to the new kernel, not deleted (coverage audit in 8f). `debruijn_vm_contract_test` (B0 normaliser) and `debruijn_register_contract_test` (R0 descriptor/address law) pin contract facts, not lowerer behaviour: port the descriptor and normaliser assertions to `debruijn_code_test`/`debruijn_register_code_test`, delete the lowerer legs.\n166:**Q1 (OWNER). Who edits the 10 C4 files, and when.** The cutover cannot compile without rewriting `linked_harness.cljc:105-120` and the ten Python tests that require the old kernel, `dl`, `rc` and `handoff`, and without regenerating `manifest-golden`, `pysp-manifest-golden` and verifying the literal-address pins. Options: (a) this track edits them in slice 8f under a frozen C4 branch state the owner declares (the other orchestrator pauses commits to those files for the slice's duration); (b) the other orchestrator ports them against a published 8c/8e API on a C4 branch, and 8f merges both. Recommendation: (a), because the change is mechanical (the harness's three backend lambdas and the loader) and a two-party edit of one file is the worst conflict shape; cost half a day plus the C4 slow lane (hours on a laptop; shiva/jupiter preferred). The owner must also say whether the C4 goldens are \"ours to move\" (derivations §8.1 says publish twice and diff; the review must show the diff).\n168:**Q2 (OWNER). Do versions 0/1/2, `handoff.cljc` and `checkpoint.cljc` die in this phase.** The brief says yes (\"leave at phase 8\"). The inventory confirms zero production requirers and 141 tests. Recommendation: yes, in one slice (8d), because keeping a 3783-line codec nobody produces is the legacy the no-compat rule forbids, and because its tests pin the old kernel and so block 8f. Cost: 1.5 days (mostly porting the helpers the new tests borrow from `lift_support`/`v2_support`). The one thing lost is the only existing test of the holder's acceptance of a version-2 body as a refusal; add one row to `rows_test` that a version-2 or version-0 header is `:yin.k/undecodable` (the holder path never dispatched on version; confirm, do not invent).\n170:**Q3. Rename mechanics for a name that other tracks and docs cite.** Recommendation: two commits inside slice 8f. Commit 1: `git mv` of 8 source and 16 test files plus `ns` forms, the `:yin.semantic-register/code` keyword to `:yin.semantic/code`, alias `sr` kept where it reads well; no logic change. Commit 2: the 56 requirers' API rewrite. Reviewers diff commit 1 with `--find-renames` and read only commit 2. Docs are a third commit (8g). Keep `yin.vm.semantic-register.*` nowhere, no alias namespace (no compat shim). Cost: 2 days codex, mostly the 58 `vm-load-program` sites.\n178:**Q7. The old harness and stage tests of the old kernel.** `engine_gate_test`, `semantic_engine_test`, `semantic_ffi_test`, `semantic_stream_observer_test`, `stream_poll_test`, `telemetry_test`, `heap_reclamation_test` etc. exercise the engine through the old kernel. Recommendation: port, do not delete; the new kernel is the same engine's client. Slice 8f carries a coverage audit: list every deftest in deleted files, and for each name a surviving deftest or write one. Reviewer gate: the suite's deftest count after 8f is at least today's minus the 85 + 141 + (91 - ported) that are tests of deleted code, documented in the slice report with the list. Cost: in 8f's 2 days.\n180:**Q8. Who regenerates the C4 goldens and the literal-address pins.** Covered by Q1; the mechanical check is derivations §8.1 row 6 (publish twice, diff the manifest map). If the five `int_literal_test` and `float_address_test` pins are v3 vector addresses they die; if tree or v4 addresses they hold. Decided at 8f by printing them.\n182:**Q9. Deletion order (irreversible).** Fixed by this plan: 8a pins and regenerates goldens and moves the H descriptor golden; 8b moves BLAKE3; 8c switches production callers; 8d deletes the old transport; 8e deletes the three old lowerers; 8f deletes the old kernel and renames; 8g documents. Nothing is deleted before its replacement is green on three hosts in the same slice or earlier.\n190:Estimates: codex implementation days; review rounds 2-4 per slice (Fable/opus, non-author). Hosts: every slice runs the quick tier on JVM per iteration (`bb test:sub:clj yin.vm` or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`), `bb test:changed` on three hosts at landing (G2). Slow tier (`bb test:all`), C4 suite with `--slow`, and three hosts once at the phase boundary (8f/8g).\n196:| **8c Production pipeline to A** | `linker.cljc:46-52` requires, `:695-731` one `semantic-format` (v4 scanners), `:2159-2177` `relowered`, `:2252-2320` `checked-derivation` (de Bruijn input = A), `:2457-2565` pairing paths; `publish.cljc:5-17, :159-213`; `ledger.cljc:40-50, :194-204`; `vm.cljc:266-269` `\"v4\"`, `:1755` footprint key; `ucf.cljc:47-68`; `completion.cljc:206, :256` (Q5); `interpreter.cljc:84-93`; `repl.cljc:27-41, :143-156, :224-240`; `rows.cljc:2118`, `semantic_register.cljc:410-456` keyword to `:yin.semantic/code` (so the keyword moves once, here, not in 8f); tests `linker_test`, `linker_manifest_test:740`, `linker_require_test`, `linker/publish_test`, `repl/serve_test:446-452`, `semantic_register/link_test`, `repl_test`, `ucf_test` (v4 vectors), `ledger_test` | 8ab | `linker-test/*` incl. `pinned-identities-are-host-independent`, `verify-same-root-pairing` tests `:1969-1980`, `linker-manifest-test/*`, `linker-require-test/*`, `publish-test/*` (manifest shape: contracts `\"v4\"`, three derivation records with de Bruijn input = A, index keyed by format), `serve-test/the-composition-registers-the-v4-format-record` (6 records), `repl-test/*`, `dht_process_test`, `remote_transfer_test`, `cross_host_transfer_test`; `derive_test/c4-execution-oracle` still green | 3 (JVM + Node REPL peer + Dart peer are all involved) | 3 d | 3-4 |\n199:| **8f Old kernel out, rename, C4 gate** | delete `semantic.cljc`, `linearize.cljc`, `code.cljc` (what remains after 8c) and `semantic_test`, `linearize_test`, `code_test`; port `semantic_engine_test`, `semantic_ffi_test`, `semantic_stream_observer_test` to the new kernel; commit 1 `git mv` `semantic_register*` to `semantic*` and `test/yin/vm/semantic_register/` to `test/yin/vm/semantic/`; commit 2 rewrite the 56 requirers (`vm-load-program` to `load-ast`/`load-vector`); demos (`continuation_handoff.cljc`, `continuation_stream.cljs`, `register_bench_cljd.cljd`, `yin_vm_bench`) ported; `linked_harness.cljc:105-120`; C4 goldens regenerated and literal pins verified (Q1, Q8); coverage audit list | 8c, 8d, 8e | **phase gate**: `bb test:all` three hosts (slow tier), `bb test:sub yang.python --slow` (C4 suite: `import_test`, `linked_prelude_test`, `linked_safepoint_test`, `safepoint_test`, `dht_import_test`, `e2e_test`, `e2e_c2_test`, `prelude_parity_test`) over newly published images = the install-child gate of derivations §6.1.4 (c); `repl-test/*`, `dht_process_test`, `store_write_audit_test` (allowlist touches); deftest-count audit per Q7; kondo clean; `lib/cljd-out` cleaned before the Dart lane | 3 + shiva/jupiter for the slow tier | 3 d | 3-4 |\n200:| **8g Documentation** | the 13 design docs, `yin.vm.semantic.md` fate (Q6), `ucf-revisions.md` record, `linker.md` §3, `build-n-test.md` if needed, the frozen design's §10 item 8 marked complete, `derivations.md` §7.4-8.3 and `continuation.md` §8.4 statuses, `debruijn.*.md` SHA-256 prose | 8f | Architect doc review; `grep -r 'yin.vm.semantic-register' docs src test` returns only historical/dated statements; `grep -rn 'sha256' src/cljc/yin/vm` returns none | n/a | 1.5 d | 2 |\n210:| 8f | 8c, 8d, 8e | nothing |\n211:| 8g | 8f | nothing (docs only; may start drafting during 8f review) |\n213:Gate list: quick tier (`bb test:sub:clj yin.vm` per iteration, `bb test:changed` three hosts at landing) for every slice; `bb test:sub yin.vm yin.vm.ucf` at 8c and 8e checkpoints; the slow tier once at 8f on shiva/jupiter plus the C4 suite with `--slow`; three hosts at every landing from 8ab up because the regenerated constants must agree on JVM, Node and Dart (that is what `pinned-identities-are-host-independent` and `register-determinism` exist for).\n221:1. **Mass rename collides with the other orchestrator.** 56 files, 10 of them C4's, and `linked_harness.cljc` is the shared hinge. Mitigation: Q1 ruling; freeze window announced via collab/owner relay before 8e starts; 8e and 8f edit C4 files only as mechanical API substitution with no behavioural change; the C4 suite over new images is the proof. Never touch `datomworld-c4-*` worktrees (none exist in `git worktree list` today; the C4 work seems to live in the main checkout's branches).\n222:2. **Accidental coverage loss when deleting 317 deftests.** Mitigation: Q7 audit list in the 8f report (deleted test name, reason: tests deleted code / ported to X / replaced by Y); port the engine-level tests; reviewer refuses 8f without the list. Count today: 4574.\n224:4. **Dart name resolution on rename.** The new `yin.vm.semantic` compiles to `lib/cljd-out/yin/vm/semantic.dart`, the same path the old kernel occupied; a stale build cache or a stale `semantic_register.dart` referenced by a stale `semantic_register/*.dart` can shadow or double-define. Mitigation: 8f's Dart lane starts with a clean `lib/cljd-out` (and the same on shiva/jupiter before their lanes); the known cljd traps (reader-conditional order, `[_ _]` protocol params, chunked `for`, unary minus) are re-checked by the three-host gate, not by inspection. Also the file rename changes the cljd build's import graph; the Dart peer binary must be rebuilt (`bb build:yin-repl-peer`) before `dht_process_test`.\n227:7. **Review-round inflation.** Phases 5-7 averaged 2-4 rounds; 8c and 8f are the two slices with the most files and the most hosts. Mitigation: split 8f into two commits (Q3) so the reviewer reads the rename as moves; keep `--find-renames` diffs in the report.\n242:| 8f | 3 | 3-4 | 5-6 days + slow tier (JVM 20+ min, Node/Dart ~5 min each, C4 slow sequence hours on a laptop; use shiva/jupiter) |\n246:Not counted: the owner's own time on Q1-Q6, and any C4-side rebase the other orchestrator needs after 8f.\n2168:### 8.4 Phase 7's entry conditions\n2184:### 8.5 Ordering and concurrency\n---\ndescription: Build, lint, test commands, TDD guidelines, and bracket debugging for Clojure, ClojureScript, Node.js, and ClojureDart\n---\n\n# BUILD & TEST\n\n## Commands\n\n```sh\n# Tests (Babashka runner)\nbb test              # All three lanes (JVM, Node, Dart), fast default form: no slow tests\nbb test:all          # Every test on every lane, slow ones included (JVM takes 20+ min)\nbb test:slow         # Only the slow tests, on every lane\n# One lane alone: <task>:clj, <task>:cljs, <task>:cljd\nbb test:clj          # JVM, fast: skips ^:slow (bare `clj -M:test` runs them too)\nbb test:cljs         # Node (shadow-cljs :cljs alias), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\nbb test:cljd         # Dart (needs flutter on PATH), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\nbb test:all:clj      # JVM, no filter: `clojure -M:test`\nbb test:all:cljs     # Node with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:all:cljd     # Dart with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:slow:clj     # JVM: `-i :slow` (long-loops-test, ~14 min)\nbb test:slow:cljs    # Node: only the namespaces that use dao.test-slow/guard, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:slow:cljd    # Dart: only those namespaces' generated tests, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\n# Slow tests: tag a deftest ^:slow. The JVM runner then excludes it from `bb test:clj`.\n# Node and Dart have no tag filter, so a .cljc slow test also wraps its body in\n# (dao.test-slow/guard \"name\" (fn [] ...)); it prints SKIP unless DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1.\n# Node and Dart select slow tests by NAMESPACE (derived by grepping `slow/guard`\n# under test/), so the non-slow tests inside those namespaces also run there.\n# On Node, shadow also runs the test namespaces those nine require (about\n# seven more, e.g. lower-portable-test); on Dart only the nine namespaces run.\n# Run test:slow / test:all before a big merge or a commit that touches many parts.\n# Run one Dart lane at a time per machine (the limit is cores and the Flutter cache,\n# not subsystems). On the many-core remote hosts see \"Remote test servers\" below for\n# which lanes may overlap.\n#\n# When to tag a test slow: when it takes more than about 5 s on any lane (the\n# Python e2e tests sit on a ~3 s floor, so their JVM cut is 3.0 s). The\n# default lanes must stay in minutes; 22 JVM tests (0.75% of the suite) were\n# 81% of its test time. Tag it ^:slow on the JVM; if it is a .cljc test, also\n# wrap its body in dao.test-slow/guard so Node and Dart skip it by default. A\n# test that is slow only on Dart (dao.jing.dht-test/unproven-chunks-... takes\n# 72 s there, 3.6 s on the JVM) is a candidate for a speed investigation first.\n# Python slow set (2026-10-06): 65 ^:slow and 15 guard-only tests in the\n# yang.python.antlr e2e, e2e-c1, prelude-parity, safepoint, float-address\n# and int-contract tests; a Python slice landing runs `bb test:sub:clj yang.python --slow`\n# (or `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>`).\n# While iterating, use the Gate Ladder (see below): G0 iterate via `bb test:sub:clj <own>`\n# or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`. Do not run the full `bb test` while iterating.\n# At slice checkpoints, run G1 (`bb test:sub <own>`). Before landing a slice, run\n# G2 (`bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes).\n# Full repo-wide `bb test` (G3) is the mandatory landing gate on master after merge.\n# Cross-host bugs show only on Node or Dart, so tri-host verification is mandatory\n# for every gate from G1 upward. Run one lane set at a time per machine to avoid\n# contention (a laptop has 8 cores; the 24- and 48-core remote hosts have room to\n# overlap lanes, see \"Remote test servers\" below).\n# Measured fast-lane times (2026-10-03): bb test about 14 min = JVM 5.5 + Node\n# 3.8 + Dart 4.3, plus builds. test:slow: Node and Dart about 5 min each (mostly\n# compile); the JVM half is estimated at 17 min (long-loops-test alone is 14),\n# not yet run end to end. test:all: JVM 20+ min, also an estimate.\nnpm test             # Node.js tests\n\n# Cross-host peers the JVM lane spawns. `bb test` builds both. A bare\n# `bb test:clj` builds only the Node REPL (it depends on build:yin-repl-node);\n# without build:yin-repl-peer first, the Dart-peer tests skip with a printed notice.\n# Under `bb test:sub` and `bb test:changed`, prerequisites are derived automatically.\nbb build:yin-repl-peer   # Dart exe build/yin-repl-peer (yin.repl R5 pairs)\nbb build:yin-repl-node   # Node REPL target/yin-repl.js; REQUIRED by\n                         # yin.repl.dht-process-test (JVM-to-Node DHT reader,\n                         # and the linker L5 Node reader that requires a module\n                         # by name): `clojure -M:test` without it fails that test\n```\n\n### Changed-only tests: `bb test:changed`\n\n`bb test:changed` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the fast tests a change can\nreach: the reverse require closure of the files changed since the\nmerge-base with master, plus fixtures that mention a changed resource path,\ntests that walk a changed tree, and each src file's `_test` by convention.\nA walker is a file calling file-seq, fs/glob, fs/list-dir,\nfs/walk-file-tree or .listFiles; each string literal argument of its\nio/file, fs/file, fs/path, fs/glob, java.io.File. or File. calls is a root\n(let-bound roots count), and a change under a root selects it. The three\nlanes run in parallel (logs: target/profile/changed-<lane>.log) after the\nneeded builds. `test:changed:list` shows what and why; `:clj`/`:cljs`/\n`:cljd` run one lane; `--base REF`; `--changed FILE...` (absolute OK; a\ntypo, an empty list or the wrong cwd exits 1). Use it to iterate and to\nland a slice confined to a package. Exit 2 (from :list too) is a wide\nchange (deps.edn bb.edn shadow-cljs.edn mise.toml .cljstyle src/dev/\nantlr/ .clj-kondo/ package*/pubspec*): it runs nothing; run `bb test`,\nas for a change to the core everything requires (yin.vm engine,\ndao.stream, codecs) and before a big merge. Not seen: dynamic loads\n(`requiring-resolve`, `resolve`), paths or walk roots built at run time,\nand spawned programs that changed (target/yin-repl.js, Dart peers).\n\n### Subsystem-first tests: `bb test:sub`\n\n`bb test:sub <subsystem>...` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the tests belonging to\none or more canonical subsystems (defined in `src/dev/subsystems.edn`). Selection\nis by longest namespace prefix matching.\n\n```sh\nbb test:sub <subsystem>...        # All three lanes in parallel (fast form)\nbb test:sub:clj <subsystem>...    # JVM lane alone\nbb test:sub:cljs <subsystem>...   # Node lane alone\nbb test:sub:cljd <subsystem>...   # Dart lane alone\nbb test:sub:list <subsystem>...   # Show namespaces, builds, Class B seams & dependents; runs nothing\nbb test:sub <subsystem>... --slow # Include slow tests (-i :slow on JVM, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1 on Node/Dart)\nbb -cp src/dev -m affected-test   # Run affected-test self-tests directly in Babashka\n```\n\n#### Canonical Subsystems (`src/dev/subsystems.edn`)\n\n- **Layer 0 (Core Data & Transport)**: `dao.stream`, `dao.jing`, `dao.data`, `dao.base`\n- **Layer 1 (Space & UI)**: `dao.space`, `dao.gui`, `dao.postgraphics`\n- **Layer 2 (Execution Engine)**: `yin.vm.ucf`, `yin.vm`\n- **Layer 3 (Languages & Interop)**: `yin.repl`, `yang.python`, `yang.base`\n- **Layer 4 (Applications & Demos)**: `demo`, `world`\n- **Infrastructure**: `dev`, `bench`\n\nPrerequisites (`build:yin-repl-peer`, `build:yin-repl-node`, `gen:python-antlr`)\nand lane serialization (`[:clj :cljd]`) are derived purely from text reach of the\nselected namespaces and run automatically when needed.\n\n#### Gate Ladder (docs/design/test.subsystems.md)\n\n1. **G0 Iterate**: Run only the subsystem's JVM lane (`bb test:sub:clj <own>` or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`).\n2. **G1 Checkpoint**: Run the three-lane subsystem test (`bb test:sub <own>`). Proves the home suite across all three hosts before reporting ready.\n3. **G2 Land**: Run `bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes (plus `bb test:sub <own> --slow` if slow tests are present). Covers dependents across the change (for foundation subsystems like `dao.stream` or `dao.jing`, this correctly runs nearly the full suite).\n4. **G3 Master**: The mandatory landing gate on master after merge, and for any wide change (`bb test`).\n5. **G4 Release**: Full or slow test run before a big release or cross-cutting merge (`bb test:slow` or `bb test:all`), and at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track (owner decision 2026-10-12; see \"Test tiers: quick and slow\" below). G0 to G3 run the quick tier; G4 runs the slow tier.\n\n\nThe linker-over-DHT end-to-end gate (docs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md,\nslice L5) is two tests:\n\n- `yin.repl.dht-process-test` (JVM lane only): real `yin.repl.main`\n  processes over real loopback UDP. A JVM publisher with a key file\n  publishes modules by name, and JVM and Node readers require them by\n  name on all four VMs. Plain Clojure in the test JVM takes the same path\n  and checks each section 9 failure as data. Ports are ephemeral, and\n  every wait is bounded. A run takes a few minutes. Run it alone with\n  `clojure -M:test -n yin.repl.dht-process-test`, after\n  `bb build:yin-repl-node`.\n- `yin.vm.linker.dht-end-to-end-test` (all three lanes): the same\n  scenario in process, over the `dao.jing.dht` test mesh seam. Dart's\n  signed-name leg runs here, because no Dart `yin.repl.main` process\n  exists to spawn. `build/yin-repl-peer` is the R5 slice peer, not a\n  REPL.\n\n```sh\n# ClojureScript / shadow-cljs (always via deps.edn :cljs alias, never npx)\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli watch <build-id>    # e.g. watch demo\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build-id>  # e.g. compile demo\nclj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli release <build-id>  # production release\n\n# ClojureDart\nclj -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\n\n# Linting\nclj -M:kondo --lint <path>\n```\n\nThe Dart `bb` lanes run `src/dev/cljd_agg.clj` (`bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj\n[--slow-only | --slow-regex]`), not `cljd test`. `--slow-only` runs only the\nnamespaces that use `dao.test-slow/guard` and fails if one has no generated\nDart file. `--slow-regex` prints those namespaces as an anchored alternation\nwith its dots already escaped for an EDN string (bb.edn feeds it to shadow-cljs\n`--config-merge` for `test:slow:cljs`); it is not a ready-to-use regex, and it\nexits 1 when no namespace uses the guard. `flutter test` spends about 7.5 s loading\neach of the ~166 generated `test/cljd-out/**/*_test.dart` files, so the\nscript compiles every test namespace via `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd compile\n<namespaces>`, writes up to min(8, cores) shard files under `build/cljd-agg/`\n(greedy by file size; each imports its files and calls their `main`), runs\n`flutter test --concurrency N` on the shards, and deletes them. Shards never sit\nunder `test/`, so plain `clojure -M:clojuredart:cljd test` still works. Tests of\ndifferent namespaces share an isolate per shard. Failures stay attributable:\npackage:test names are ns-qualified (`dao.foo-test/bar-test`).\n\n`flutter test`/`cljd test` globs everything under `test/cljd-out/`, and the\ncompile does not prune it when a source file is deleted or renamed. The\naggregated lanes only run namespaces that still exist under `test/`, but a\nstale file can silently pass a plain `cljd test`. Run `rm -rf test/cljd-out`\nbefore relying on that to prove a deletion or rename took effect.\n\n## Test tiers: quick and slow\n\nThe suite has two tiers so that slow tests run less often than quick ones\n(owner decision, 2026-10-12).\n\n- **Quick tier:** every test not tagged `^:slow`. `bb test:clj` or\n  `clojure -M:test -e :slow` on the JVM; the Node and Dart lanes skip the\n  guarded bodies (`dao.test-slow/guard`) unless `DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1`.\n- **Slow tier:** the tagged deftests. `bb test:slow` or\n  `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>` on the JVM.\n- **The tagging rule** is the one above (\"When to tag a test slow\"): a deftest that\n  takes about 5 s or more run alone. On 2026-10-12 every JVM test namespace and every\n  deftest in the 22 slowest namespaces were timed on a 48-core Linux server, and the\n  **63 deftests at 5 s or more** were tagged: `^:slow` on the deftest, plus the guard\n  wrapper in `.cljc` files. 37 are guarded, 26 are `^:slow` only (`.clj` files and\n  `:clj` reader-conditional branches). Those 22 namespaces now hold **168 slow tests**\n  in all (some were already tagged). Per-test times are not in the repo: measure again\n  with the method below before changing the cut.\n- **Cadence:** the quick tier at every checkpoint (G1) and landing (G2, G3); the slow\n  tier before merging a big change and at each phase boundary of a multi-phase track\n  (the existing G4). There is no nightly run.\n\nMeasured (2026-10-12, origin master plus the tags, Linux servers; see \"Remote test\nservers\" for how to run them):\n\n| Run | Time |\n|---|---|\n| Full JVM lane, one process, before tagging (shiva) | 70 min |\n| Full JVM lane, sharded by subsystem, before tagging (jupiter, 13 shards, 8 at a time) | 46.5 min, set by `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` |\n| **Quick tier, sharded by subsystem (jupiter, same 13 shards)** | **5 min 3 s**, 4055 tests, all shards exit 0; longest shards `yang.python` 253 s and `yin.vm.ucf` 204 s |\n| **Slow tier (shiva, one JVM per namespace, 6 at a time)** | **53 min**, 22 namespaces, 168 tests, all exit 0; longest `yin.repl.python-antlr-test` 3183 s (about 1580 s alone on the laptop) |\n\nThe quick tier has exactly 63 fewer tests than the untagged suite (4118 to 4055).\nSome quick parts are still 25 to 69 s when a namespace is run alone (`e2e-c2`,\n`prelude-parity`, `int-ops`, `holder.driver`, `compose-rows`): many tests each just\nunder the 5 s cut (the Python e2e per-test floor is about 2 to 3 s), not shared setup.\nA lower cut would shrink the quick tier further and move more tests to the slow tier.\n\n**How the per-test times were measured.** To time every deftest of a namespace alone,\nrun it without the test runner's alias main (the `:test` alias's `-m cognitect.test-runner`\notherwise swallows `-e`, and every JVM runs the whole suite):\n\n```sh\nNS=<namespace> clojure -Sdeps '{:aliases {:t {:extra-paths [\"test\"]}}}' -M:t timing.clj\n# timing.clj: require the namespace, for each deftest var that is not ^:slow:\n#   (binding [clojure.test/*test-out* (java.io.StringWriter.)] (clojure.test/test-vars [v]))\n# and print the elapsed nanoseconds. One-off setup (a `use-fixtures :once` or a parser\n# load) is paid by the first test in file order; check before tagging that test alone.\n```\n\n**Sharding the JVM lane by subsystem** (the quick tier; run on a server, see below). The\nshards come from `bb test:sub:list <subsystem>` for the 13 subsystems that hold JVM\ntests, so each subsystem is one JVM; the script also checks that the shards cover every\nJVM test namespace under `test/`:\n\n```sh\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Usage: shard-jvm.sh <clone> [parallel]  -- JVM lane sharded by subsystem (fast tests, -e :slow)\nset -u\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\ncd \"$1\" || exit 2\nPAR=\"${2:-8}\"\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\nOUT=\"$1/target-ci/shards\"; rm -rf \"$OUT\"; mkdir -p \"$OUT\"\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\necho \"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\" | tee \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nnpm ci --silent >\"$OUT/npm.log\" 2>&1                   && echo \"npm ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\"$OUT/build-node.log\" 2>&1     && echo \"node repl built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nbb gen:python-antlr >\"$OUT/antlr.log\" 2>&1             && echo \"antlr ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nSUBS=\"yang.python yin.repl yin.vm yin.vm.ucf dao.stream dao.jing dao.gui dao.space demo dao.data dao.postgraphics yang.base dao.base\"\n: > \"$OUT/covered.txt\"\nfor s in $SUBS; do\n  bb test:sub:list \"$s\" 2>/dev/null | awk \"/^clj:/{f=1;next} /^[A-Za-z]/{f=0} f{gsub(/ /,\\\"\\\");print}\" > \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\"\n  cat \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\" >> \"$OUT/covered.txt\"\n  echo \"$s $(wc -l < \"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\")\" >> \"$OUT/plan.txt\"\ndone\n# coverage: every JVM test namespace under test/ vs the union of the shards\nfind test -type f \\( -name \"*_test.clj\" -o -name \"*_test.cljc\" \\) | sed -e \"s#^test/##\" -e \"s#\\.cljc\\?\\$##\" -e \"s#/#.#g\" -e \"s#_#-#g\" | sort -u > \"$OUT/all.txt\"\nsort -u \"$OUT/covered.txt\" > \"$OUT/covered.sorted\"\ncomm -23 \"$OUT/all.txt\" \"$OUT/covered.sorted\" > \"$OUT/ns-uncovered.txt\"\necho \"all=$(wc -l < $OUT/all.txt) covered=$(wc -l < $OUT/covered.sorted) uncovered=$(wc -l < $OUT/ns-uncovered.txt)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nrun_shard() {\n  s=$1; f=\"$OUT/ns-$s.txt\"; [ -s \"$f\" ] || return 0\n  args=(); while read -r n; do args+=(-n \"$n\"); done < \"$f\"\n  t0=$(date +%s); clojure -M:test -e :slow \"${args[@]}\" >\"$OUT/$s.log\" 2>&1; rc=$?\n  echo \"$s rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(ts) $(grep -E \"^Ran \" \"$OUT/$s.log\" | tail -1)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n}\nexport -f run_shard; export OUT; export -f ts\nprintf \"%s\\n\" $SUBS uncovered | xargs -P \"$PAR\" -I{} bash -c \"run_shard {}\"\necho \"done $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n```\n\n**The slow tier** runs each tagged namespace on its own JVM, a few at a time:\n\n```sh\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Usage: slow-tier.sh <clone> [parallel] -- the ^:slow tests of the tagged namespaces, one JVM per namespace\nset -u\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\ncd \"$1\" || exit 2\nPAR=\"${2:-6}\"\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\nOUT=\"$1/target-ci/slowtier\"; rm -rf \"$OUT\"; mkdir -p \"$OUT\"\nrun_ns() {\n  n=$1; t0=$(date +%s)\n  clojure -M:test -i :slow -n \"$n\" > \"$OUT/$n.log\" 2>&1; rc=$?\n  echo \"$n rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-t0 )) $(grep -E \"^Ran \" \"$OUT/$n.log\" | tail -1)\" >> \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n}\nexport -f run_ns; export OUT\necho \"start $(date +%H:%M:%S) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD) par=$PAR\" > \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\nxargs -P \"$PAR\" -I{} bash -c \"run_ns {}\" < \"$HOME/ci/pertest-ns.txt\"\necho \"done $(date +%H:%M:%S)\" >> \"$OUT/summary.txt\"\n```\n\n## Remote test servers: shiva and jupiter\n\nTwo Linux x86_64 servers run the full suite faster than a laptop. Both are\nreached over SSH as `sto` with key authentication, and both have `mise`, `tmux`\nand `git`.\n\n| Host | Cores / RAM | Toolchain state (checked 2026-10-11) |\n|---|---|---|\n| `shiva.stigmergy.systems` | 24 / 62 GB | Complete: java 21, clojure 1.12.4.1602, node 25.6.1, babashka, cljstyle, zprint, flutter 3.47.1-stable. All three lanes run. |\n| `jupiter.stigmergy.systems` | 48 / 62 GB | java 21, clojure, node, zprint; babashka installed with `mise install babashka@latest`; flutter 3.41.9 is NOT the pinned version. Run the JVM and Node lanes here, not Dart. |\n\n**Never test in `~/workspace/datomworld` on either host.** It is a working copy\nthat is behind origin (shiva: 45 commits behind on 2026-10-11) and belongs to its\nowner. Use a scratch clone under `~/ci/` and leave the working copy alone.\n\n### Get the code there: push to origin, never rsync\n\nRsyncing a worktree over the WAN ran at about 150 KB/s (576 MB of tracked files\nwould take about an hour), and an unanchored `--exclude build` also dropped source\ndirectories named `build`, `out` and `target`. Both hosts reach GitHub, so push the\nbranch from the laptop (a feature branch, never master), make one scratch clone on\nthe host from its existing checkout (a local clone takes seconds), and fetch:\n\n```sh\n# once per host: a separate scratch clone, not the working copy\nssh shiva.stigmergy.systems 'set -e; mkdir -p ~/ci; cd ~/workspace/datomworld;\n  URL=$(git remote get-url origin);\n  git clone -q --no-checkout ~/workspace/datomworld ~/ci/datomworld-<name>;\n  cd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>; git remote set-url origin \"$URL\";\n  git fetch -q origin; git checkout -q --detach origin/<branch>'\n```\n\nTo test another branch later, fetch and check it out in the scratch clone with\n`git fetch origin` then `git checkout --detach origin/<branch>`. A branch that\nwas never pushed cannot be tested remotely.\n\n### Activate the toolchain in the right order\n\nA non-interactive `ssh host cmd` does not load the shell profile, so `mise` and its\ntools are not on PATH. In a script, do this in exactly this order, after the `cd`\ninto the clone:\n\n```sh\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"   # mise: ~/.local/bin on shiva, /snap/bin on jupiter\ncd ~/ci/datomworld-<name>\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1                       # trust is per path; a fresh clone is untrusted\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"                       # puts the repo-pinned tools on PATH\n```\n\nActivating mise before the `cd` and `mise trust` leaves the repo-pinned tools\n(flutter in particular) off PATH: the Dart lane then dies in seconds with \"Can't\nfind flutter on PATH\", and so does `bb build:yin-repl-peer`.\n\n### Which lanes may overlap (reconciling the one-lane-set rule)\n\n\"One lane set at a time\" and \"one Dart lane at a time\" are per-machine rules about\nCPU: they exist because a laptop has 8 cores. They are not a rule about repos.\n`test.subsystems.md` section 5 item 6 says the same (\"on one machine where\navoidable\"). On shiva (24 cores) and jupiter (48) the cores are not the limit, so\nlanes may overlap, with these limits:\n\n- **JVM and Dart lanes must not share one clone.** `src/dev/affected.clj`\n  (`serialized-pairs`, the `[:clj :cljd]` pair) serializes them whenever a JVM test\n  runs the cljd compiler, and some do (`yin.repl.build-test`,\n  `yin.vm.linker.cross-host-transfer-test`, `dao.stream.ws-project-cross-jvm-test`);\n  the compiler writes the clone's `lib/cljd-out` and `build/`. A second scratch clone\n  gives the Dart lane its own copies, so the two lanes then overlap safely. This is\n  by the design's own reasoning (\"each worktree owns its own `cljd-out`\"), not yet\n  verified by a measured run.\n- **JVM and Node may share a clone.** Node writes `target/` and `.shadow-cljs/`; the\n  JVM lane only reads `target/yin-repl.js`.\n- **One Dart lane per host at a time**, even across clones: the lane shards up to\n  `min(8, cores)` Flutter processes and shares the host's Flutter cache.\n- **Different branches in different clones** are independent, so two branches may\n  run on one host if its cores and RAM allow (62 GB each).\n- Do not treat this as permission to overlap lanes on the laptop.\n\nThe first run on shiva (2026-10-11) overlapped JVM and Dart in one clone before\nthis rule was written down; its results are advisory until repeated with a\nseparate Dart clone.\n\n### The full run\n\nRun long jobs inside `tmux` so the SSH session can drop. The script below does the\nserial prerequisites once, then runs the JVM and Node lanes in the first clone and\nthe Dart lane in a second clone, all concurrently, with the same commands the `bb`\ntasks use (the `bb test` task runs them one after another), and records each\nlane's exit code and seconds. Make the Dart clone the same way as the first, at the\nsame commit, with a name like `~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart`:\n\n```sh\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# ~/ci/run-full.sh <jvm-node-clone> [<dart-clone>]   (omit the second on jupiter: no Dart)\nset -u\nexport PATH=\"$HOME/.local/bin:/snap/bin:$PATH\"\nDART=\"${2:-}\"\ncd \"$1\" || exit 2\nmise trust >/dev/null 2>&1\neval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\nLOG=\"$1/target-ci\"; mkdir -p \"$LOG\"\nts() { date +%H:%M:%S; }\necho \"start $(ts) $(git rev-parse --short HEAD)\" | tee \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nnpm ci --silent >\"$LOG/npm.log\" 2>&1               && echo \"npm ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nbb build:yin-repl-node >\"$LOG/build-node.log\" 2>&1 && echo \"node repl built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nbb gen:python-antlr >\"$LOG/antlr.log\" 2>&1         && echo \"antlr ok $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\nbb build:yin-repl-peer >\"$LOG/build-peer.log\" 2>&1 && echo \"dart peer built $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"  # shiva only\nlane() { name=$1; shift; s=$(date +%s); \"$@\" >\"$LOG/$name.log\" 2>&1; rc=$?\n         echo \"$name rc=$rc secs=$(( $(date +%s)-s )) $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"; }\nlane jvm  clojure -M:test -e :slow &\nlane node clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile test &\nif [ -n \"$DART\" ]; then                            # shiva only: its own clone (own lib/cljd-out, build/)\n  ( cd \"$DART\" && mise trust >/dev/null 2>&1 && eval \"$(mise env -s bash)\"\n    bb build:yin-repl-peer >\"$LOG/build-peer-dart.log\" 2>&1\n    lane dart bb src/dev/cljd_agg.clj ) &\nfi\nwait\necho \"done $(ts)\" | tee -a \"$LOG/summary.txt\"\n```\n\n```sh\nssh <host> 'tmux new-session -d -s ci-full \"bash -lc \\\"~/ci/run-full.sh ~/ci/datomworld-<name> ~/ci/datomworld-<name>-dart > ~/ci/run-full.out 2>&1\\\"\"'\n```\n\nWatch it: `ssh <host>`, then `tmux attach -t ci-full` (detach with `Ctrl-b d`; do not\npress Ctrl-C inside it), or `tail -f ~/ci/datomworld-<name>/target-ci/{jvm,node,dart}.log`\nand `cat .../summary.txt` (all logs and the summary are in the first clone). The session's own output is empty by design: the lanes\nwrite to the logs. The JVM lane uses `-e :slow`, so it skips `^:slow` tests, like\n`bb test:clj`. For the slow set use `-i :slow` (or `bb test:slow`); the slow C4\nscanner row took about 11-19 minutes on the JVM and about 79 minutes on Node\n(measured 2026-10-11).\n\n### Known differences from the laptop\n\n- **`dao.stream.datagram.dart-test/refusals-and-lifecycle-events` fails on Linux.**\n  The assertion \"a bind on a taken port deposits bind-failed\" gets no `bind-failed`\n  event. A second UDP bind on a taken port evidently behaves differently on Linux\n  than on macOS (inferred, not confirmed). The test is in `dao.stream`, which the\n  semantic-register slices do not touch: treat it as a known Linux-only failure,\n  not a regression, until it is fixed or guarded.\n- A JVM lane started before the Dart peer is built skips the Dart-peer tests with a\n  printed notice. The script builds the peer first so they run.\n- Both hosts run other work (a QEMU guest and rustdesk on shiva); idle load is\n  under 1, but timings are not a clean benchmark.\n\n### Measured times (first runs, 2026-10-11, commit 84c7c9b7, all lanes concurrent)\n\nShiva, Dart lane: 1054 s (17.5 min) cold, with the JVM and Node lanes running beside\nit, against about 9 minutes on the 8-core laptop run alone. Prerequisites (`npm ci`,\nNode REPL, ANTLR) took about 30 s; the Dart peer 91 s. JVM and Node lane times are\nnot recorded yet: fill them in from `summary.txt` after a completed run. Running the\nlanes concurrently on one host did not make the Dart lane faster, so count on the\nextra cores and a free laptop, and measure before relying on a speedup.\n\n## Testing Philosophy & TDD\n\nWrite tests before implementing features (TDD).\n\nTests define the contract and expected behavior. Implementation follows from the tests, not the other way around.\n\nWhen implementing a feature or fix:\n1. **Red phase**: Write the test first — define what should happen.\n2. **Green phase**: Implement the minimum code to pass the test.\n3. **Refactor phase**: Refactor if needed while keeping tests green.\n\nThis ensures code is testable by design and implementation matches actual requirements.\n\n## Debugging Malformed CLJ / EDN / CLJS\n\nA single missing bracket can silently break an entire file. The compiler may still succeed, with subsequent forms nesting inside the broken one.\n\n1. **Run clj-kondo first**:\n   ```sh\n   clj -M:kondo --lint path/to/file.cljs\n   ```\n\n2. **If clj-kondo reports a mismatch, write a bracket-tracing script**:\n   Do NOT try to eyeball-count brackets. Track a stack of `(char, line, col)` for every opener. Handle: string literals, escaped chars, and `;;` line comments.\n\n3. **Fix one bracket at a time, re-run checker after each fix**:\n   Common patterns:\n   - Hiccup vector missing `]` causes siblings to become extra arguments.\n   - `defn`/`let` missing `)` causes subsequent top-level forms to nest inside.\n   - Both can coexist while total bracket count is zero-sum.\n\n4. **After brackets balance**, rerun `clj-kondo`, then rebuild with `clj -M:cljs -m shadow.cljs.devtools.cli compile <build>`.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '145,185p' collab/1792050511876-architect-8f1-ruling.report.md; sed -n '1360,1435p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md; rg -l 'yin.vm.(semantic|linearize|code)(\\\\s|\\\\])' src test; cat src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf.cljc | head -90; cat deps.edn | head -80\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '145,185p' collab/1792050511876-architect-8f1-ruling.report.md; sed -n '1360,1435p' docs/design/yin.vm.semantic-register-vm.continuation.md; rg -l 'yin.vm.(semantic|linearize|code)(\\\\s|\\\\])' src test; cat src/cljc/yin/vm/ucf.cljc | head -90; cat deps.edn | head -80\"","aggregated_output":"logical names, so the procedure's inputs no longer depend on any lane's\nidentities. Guest sets and maps are entered in the bytewise order of\ntheir members' keys, and `:store`, `:bindings` and the parked table in\nthe byte order of their structural keys (draft §6 W1), never in host\nenumeration order, so\na permutation of the erased identities cannot change the result once\nthe markers are substituted. It is never used outside the harness.\n\n**Amended after 6c (2026-10-13), positions carry identities and what\n`canonical-under` may erase (codex D1, accepted verbatim by R4;\nreplacing the claim above that after marker substitution the\nprocedure's inputs no longer depend on any lane's identities).** A\ncursor position may contain a stream identity. The fixture supplies an\nexplicit, injective logical-stream correspondence and a\ntransport-specific position renaming operation. That operation replaces\nonly identity-bearing fields, preserves every nonidentity field\nincluding offsets, and rejects missing coverage or disagreement with the\ncursor body's stream. Stream descriptors must be shown equivalent under\nthe same correspondence before comparison; replacing a whole descriptor\nwith a logical name alone does not prove equivalence. After these\nchecks, canonical-under re-emits and renumbers the task. The harness\nincludes negative controls proving that a changed position offset,\ndescriptor nonidentity field, saved value or lexical value changes the\ncompared state, and asserts exactly the three distinct kernel lanes for\nevery law-4 case. *What the code does:* `canonical-under` erases exactly\none thing, a stream marker's identity and descriptor, replaced by the\nlogical name (`rows.cljc:971-974`); a `dao.stream.ringbuffer` cursor\nposition embeds its stream's identity, which that erasure does not\nreach. The harness's `logical-positions` rewrites only\n`:dao.stream.ringbuffer/identity` inside each `:cursor-cell-body`'s\nposition under `ring-names` (resource key per stream identity) and keeps\nevery other field, the offset included; `logical-names` (served identity\nto resource key) and `ring-names` both pass through `injective`, which\nthrows `{:law-4 :injective}` when two identities share a name;\n`task-root` asserts that no lane identity survives in the emitted rows;\n`logical-descriptors` renames the identities inside each stream's\n`stream/descriptor` under the same correspondence and\n`canonical-equality` throws `{:law-4 :descriptors}` unless the three\nlanes' maps are equal; `canonical-equality` asserts `(= 3 (count\nroots))` and `(= [:semantic-register :stack :register] (mapv :lane\nroots))` for every case. `harness/law-4-controls` (quick tier, row 1\nbare) pins: a cursor position's offset incremented on one lane changes\nthe root; a stream only the register lane holds fails with\n`:descriptors`; two served identities under one logical name fail with\n`:injective`; a saved window value and a lexical value replaced on one\nlane each change the root. *Why:* codex M11 (compare one lane) and M12\n(replace every position by a constant) survived the suite, and probe 4\nshowed a descriptor field added on one lane was invisible to\n`canonical-under`; the erasure is design-sanctioned and stays, and what\nit cannot prove the harness proves separately (R4).\n\n### 5.6 Static checks the harness runs once per corpus segment\n\n1. `frame/defect` on every frame the reference lane mints: nil.\n2. For every **reachable** parking pc `p` of `v`: `(analysis/saved a\n   (inc p) rd)` has no member `≥ k(body(p))` and does not contain `rd`.\n3. Register layout: at every reachable boundary pc `p`, `virt(p, s)` is\n   single-valued on the physical image of `live-out*(p)`; the `live`\n   operand of `R[p]` equals `(sort (map phys (disj live-out*(p) rd)))`,\n   the destination removed as a **virtual** id before mapping, which is\n   `[]` for a tail call (phase-5 §2.5). Worked example, inner call at\n   `p = 3`: `L(4) = {1 2}`, minus `rd 2` → `{1}` → `[0]`, the pinned\n   `live` of phase-5 §6.3 row 2.\n4. Stack layout: the k-th non-push instruction of `H` has the mnemonic\n   family of `v[k]` for every `k`; counts agree.\n5. Stack layout: `(set (stack-layout p)) = (analysis/saved a (inc p)\n   rd)` for every reachable **non-tail** parking pc `p`.\n6. `E` reconstruction: at every safepoint reached, the layout's `E`\n   equals the reference lane's `E` (values normalised), including rows\n   21 and 22 and the `:duplicate-param`, `:nested-closure`,\n   `:nested-lambdas` programs.\n7. Closure reconstruction: canonical ↔ positional closures round-trip on\n   the `\"closure value\"` and `\"higher order\"` B0 rows and\n   `:duplicate-param`.\n8. Tail lowering: the `:frames`/`:store-of` chosen for a lowered `tail`\n   entry are unobservable: lowering with `[]`/`nil` and with arbitrary\ntest/yin/vm/rule_r_test.cljc\ntest/datomworld/demo/continuation_handoff_test.cljc\ntest/yin/vm/code_test.cljc\ntest/yin/vm/attach_image_test.cljc\ntest/yin/vm/telemetry_test.cljc\ntest/yin/vm/semantic_test.cljc\ntest/yin/vm/linearize_test.cljc\ntest/yin/vm/ffi_test.cljc\ntest/yin/vm/test_utils.cljc\ntest/yin/vm/host_typed_values_test.cljc\ntest/yin/vm/effect_forgery_test.cljc\ntest/yin/vm/stream_poll_test.cljc\ntest/yin/vm/cell_test.cljc\ntest/yin/vm/heap_reclamation_test.cljc\ntest/yang/python/antlr/safepoint_test.cljc\ntest/yin/vm/data_test.cljc\ntest/yin/vm/debruijn/register_test.cljc\ntest/yang/python/antlr/float_address_test.cljc\ntest/yin/vm/debruijn/stack_effects_test.cljc\ntest/yin/vm/debruijn/stack_parity_test.cljc\ntest/yang/python/antlr/e2e_test.clj\ntest/yang/python/antlr/int_heap_test.cljc\ntest/yin/vm/semantic_engine_test.cljc\ntest/dao/space/query_test.cljc\ntest/yang/python/antlr/prelude_parity_test.cljc\ntest/yang/python/antlr/int_literal_test.cljc\ntest/yang/python/antlr/e2e_c2_test.clj\ntest/yang/python/antlr/int_ops_test.cljc\nsrc/cljs/datomworld/demo/continuation_stream.cljs\ntest/yin/vm_test.cljc\ntest/bench/yin_vm_bench.cljc\ntest/yang/python/antlr/int_contract_test.cljc\ntest/yin/vm/semantic_stream_observer_test.cljc\ntest/yin/vm/ucf/remote_test.cljc\ntest/yin/vm/continuation_invoke_test.cljc\nsrc/cljd/yin/register_bench_cljd.cljd\ntest/yin/vm/ffi/remote_serve/responder_test.cljc\ntest/yin/vm/engine_gate_test.cljc\ntest/yin/vm/integer_test.cljc\ntest/yin/vm/semantic_ffi_test.cljc\ntest/yin/vm/ucf_test.cljc\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/ucf.cljc\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc\nsrc/cljc/datomworld/demo/continuation_handoff.cljc\n(ns yin.vm.ucf\n  \"Universal Continuation Format, Phase 1: code identity and safepoints\n   (`docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md` S7.3, S7.4).\n\n   Two static halves of the format live here, both pure functions over\n   data:\n\n   - S7.3 code identity. `batch->canonical-instruction-vector` reads a\n     well-formed `:yin.code/*` segment batch through the loader's resolved\n     interpretation (index-batch collapse, last value wins) and produces\n     the positional instruction tuple vector -- pc is the index, each tuple\n     is its mnemonic plus exactly the S2.4 operands in table order, every\n     ref a resolved pc, every defaulted operand materialized. The address\n     of that vector is `(dao.jing/segment-key v)`; the execution contract\n     it is interpreted under is `contract-stamp`. `canonicalize` bundles\n     the three into one S7.9 outcome map.\n\n   - S7.4 safepoints. `safepoints` derives, from the canonical vector\n     alone, the static half of the safepoint map a foreign engine\n     publishes: the parking transitions, their canonical resume pcs, the\n     stack effect across the parking instruction, and the names the code\n     reachable from the resume pc can read from E. The dynamic half --\n     absolute depth, captured bindings, activation bases -- is never\n     derived: `activation-state` reads it off a frame, which is the only\n     place it exists.\n\n   Content addressing is delegated entirely to `dao.jing`; nothing here\n   knows how a vector is serialized.\"\n  (:require [dao.jing :as jing]\n            [dao.jing.cbor :as cbor]\n            [yin.vm :as vm]\n            [yin.vm.code :as code]))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; The execution contract (S7.3.3)\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(def contract-stamp\n  \"The complete execution contract a canonical vector is interpreted under:\n   `:yin.code/contract` names the published revision of the tuple grammar,\n   S2.6 rules, last-value-wins resolution, opcode table, transitions,\n   resolution precedence, effect outcomes and scheduler\n   (`yin.vm.semantic.md` S2.4); `:yin.k/version` is this envelope's own\n   version (the rows execution profile is version 1). A resumer on another revision answers `:yin.k/profile-mismatch`\n   before lowering; a code index is keyed per stamp.\"\n  {:yin.code/contract vm/semantic-contract,\n   :yin.k/version 1})\n\n\n(def profiles\n  \"The version-2 execution profile registry (UCF v2 amendment, section 1):\n   the closed set of exact `:yin.k/contract` maps a version-2 body may\n   carry, by engine.  The engine names are literal unqualified keywords,\n   not the kernels' runtime `:format`s; walker and semantic share the\n   contract string `v3` and differ by engine alone.  `contract-stamp`\n   stays the frozen version-0/1 stamp.\"\n  {:semantic {:yin.k/engine :semantic\n              :yin.code/contract vm/semantic-contract\n              :yin.k/version 1}\n   :stack {:yin.k/engine :stack\n           :yin.code/contract vm/stack-contract\n           :yin.k/version 1}\n   :register {:yin.k/engine :register\n              :yin.code/contract vm/register-contract\n              :yin.k/version 1}\n   :walker {:yin.k/engine :walker\n            :yin.code/contract vm/ast-contract\n            :yin.k/version 1}})\n\n\n(def supported-profiles\n  \"Every profile map a version-2 reader of this composition accepts.\"\n  (set (vals profiles)))\n\n\n(defn profile-engine\n  \"The engine of `profile` when it is exactly one registry entry, the\n   inner version compared on the canonical integer kind so a float\n   carrier never equals its integral value; nil otherwise.\"\n  [profile]\n  (when (and (map? profile) (= 3 (count profile)))\n    (let [engine (:yin.k/engine profile)\n          p (get profiles engine)\n          v (:yin.k/version profile)]\n      (when (and p\n                 (= (:yin.code/contract p) (:yin.code/contract profile))\n                 (cbor/numeric? v)\n                 (= :integer (cbor/numeric-kind v))\n                 (cbor/num= v (:yin.k/version p)))\n{:deps {org.clojure/core.async {:mvn/version \"1.8.741\"}\n        org.clojure/tools.reader {:mvn/version \"1.5.0\"}\n        ring/ring {:mvn/version \"1.14.1\"}\n        com.cognitect/transit-clj {:mvn/version \"1.0.333\"}\n        org.replikativ/boring {:mvn/version \"0.1.30\"}\n        com.datomic/peer {:mvn/version \"1.0.7364\"}\n        org.postgresql/postgresql {:mvn/version \"42.7.3\"}\n        org.clojure/data.json {:mvn/version \"2.5.0\"}\n        http-kit/http-kit {:mvn/version \"2.8.0\"}\n        org.jsoup/jsoup {:mvn/version \"1.18.3\"}\n        instaparse/instaparse {:mvn/version \"1.5.0\"}\n        ;; yang.python.antlr: the runtime the generated Python3 parser runs\n        ;; on; pinned to the tool version in :antlr-gen (they are one release)\n        org.antlr/antlr4-runtime {:mvn/version \"4.13.2\"}\n        criterium/criterium {:mvn/version \"0.4.6\"}\n        io.github.rctcwyvrn/blake3 {:mvn/version \"1.3\"}\n        stigmergy/chp {:mvn/version \"0.1.0-SNAPSHOT\"\n                       :exclusions [refactor-nrepl/refactor-nrepl]}}\n :cljd/opts {}\n ;; build/antlr/python3/classes: the generated Python3 parser, produced by\n ;; `clj -M:antlr-gen` (antlr/python3/manifest.edn); absent until generated\n :paths [\"src/clj\" \"src/cljc\" \"src/cljs\" \"src/cljd\"\n         \"build/antlr/python3/classes\"]\n :aliases {:clean {:extra-paths [\"src/dev\"]\n                   :exec-fn clean/clean}\n           :profile-btree {:extra-paths [\"src/dev\"]\n                           :extra-deps {persistent-sorted-set/persistent-sorted-set\n                                        {:mvn/version \"0.3.0\"}}\n                           :main-opts [\"-m\" \"btree-bench\"]}\n           :test {:extra-paths [\"test\"]\n                  :extra-deps {io.github.cognitect-labs/test-runner\n                               {:git/tag \"v0.5.1\" :git/sha \"dfb30dd\"}}\n                  :main-opts [\"-m\" \"cognitect.test-runner\"]\n                  :exec-fn cognitect.test-runner.api/test}\n           :nREPL {:extra-paths [\"test\"]\n                   :extra-deps {nrepl/nrepl {:mvn/version \"1.0.0\"}\n                                cider/cider-nrepl {:mvn/version \"0.58.0\"}}\n                   :main-opts [\"-m\" \"nrepl.cmdline\" \"--middleware\" \"[cider.nrepl/cider-middleware]\"]}\n           :cljs {:extra-paths [\"test\" \"src/dev\"]\n                  :extra-deps {thheller/shadow-cljs {:mvn/version \"3.3.5\"}\n                               reagent/reagent {:mvn/version \"2.0.1\"}\n                               com.cognitect/transit-cljs {:mvn/version \"0.8.280\"}}}\n           :dev {:extra-paths [\"src/dev\"]\n                 :extra-deps {thheller/shadow-cljs {:mvn/version \"3.3.5\"}\n                              cider/cider-nrepl {:mvn/version \"0.58.0\"}\n                              reagent/reagent {:mvn/version \"2.0.1\"}\n                              com.cognitect/transit-cljs {:mvn/version \"0.8.280\"}\n                              ;; benchmark baseline + psset-fixtures\n                              ;; generator only; production uses\n                              ;; dao.data.btree\n                              persistent-sorted-set/persistent-sorted-set\n                              {:mvn/version \"0.3.0\"}}\n                 :jvm-opts [\"-Dconfig=config.clj\"]}\n           ;; The one ClojureDart pin. Compose it with the build aliases:\n           ;; clj -M:clojuredart:cljd ..., clj -M:clojuredart:cljd-yin-repl-build ...\n           :clojuredart {:extra-deps {sonwh98/clojuredart\n                                      {:git/url \"https://github.com/sonwh98/ClojureDart.git\"\n                                       :sha \"7d00ee2aa7fa9d990581e78ebc4a66b93cb70a31\"}\n                                      }}\n           :cljd {:extra-paths [\"test\"]\n                  :main-opts [\"-m\" \"cljd.build\"]\n                  :cljd/opts {:kind :flutter :main datomworld.demo.main}}\n           :server {:extra-deps {http-kit/http-kit {:mvn/version \"2.8.0\"}}\n                    :jvm-opts [\"-Dconfig=config.clj\"]\n                    :main-opts [\"-m\" \"world.server\"]}\n           :clj-yin-repl {:main-opts [\"-m\" \"yin.repl.main\"]}\n           :cljs-yin-repl\n           {:extra-paths [\"test\" \"src/dev\"]\n            :extra-deps {thheller/shadow-cljs {:mvn/version \"3.3.5\"}\n                         reagent/reagent {:mvn/version \"2.0.1\"}\n                         com.cognitect/transit-cljs {:mvn/version \"0.8.280\"}}\n            :main-opts [\"-m\" \"shadow.cljs.devtools.cli\" \"run\" \"yin.repl.main/-main\"]}\n           :fmt {:extra-paths [\"src/dev\"]\n                 :exec-fn tasks/fix-all}\n           ;; Build-time generation of the grammars-v4 Python3 parser.\n           ;; The tool version must equal the antlr4-runtime above.\n           :antlr-gen {:extra-paths [\"src/dev\"]\n                       :extra-deps {org.antlr/antlr4 {:mvn/version \"4.13.2\"}}\n                       :main-opts [\"-m\" \"yang-antlr-gen\"]}\n           :kondo {:extra-deps {clj-kondo/clj-kondo {:mvn/version \"2026.01.19\"}}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
